{"about":"e8cdaadd3471f4ba692c01a34539cbe9e587fb6075ffa3fb887ab2f3fb9404d2","body":"The conjecture creates a false dichotomy between 'strict rationality' and 'pragmatic imperative,' suggesting Popper must choose one. However, Popper's philosophy integrates these by arguing that the rational act of criticism inherently guides action. To claim that acting on the best-tested theory requires an 'extra-logical leap' implies that science is disconnected from practice, whereas Popper viewed the application of science as the ultimate test of its rationality. The preference is defensible on Popper's terms precisely because it avoids induction without resorting to irrationality; it relies on the logical asymmetry between verification and falsification.\n[would settle: An analysis showing whether Popper considered the decision to act on a corroborated theory as part of the 'context of discovery' (psychological) or the 'context of justification' (logical).]","kind":"mini.criticism.v1"}